Falsifies `paging::x86_64::the_user_va_gate_admits_only_the_aligned_low_half`.

The defect: the gate names the wrong half. This is the confinement break stated in
its shortest possible form, and it is here rather than in a subtler place because
one of the three ISA records should be the blunt one: with it, a user program may
ask the kernel to map a page at a kernel virtual address, and every downstream
check that trusts `is_user_page_va` to have already decided that question is now
deciding nothing.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(is_user_page_va::<Ia32e>(va), va & 0xfff == 0 && va
>> 47 == 0)`, on any aligned address in either half, and
`assert!(Ia32e::in_half(Half::Low, va) && !Ia32e::in_half(Half::High, va))` on any
aligned high-half address.

**The gate is shared and the proof is not.** `is_user_page_va` is one generic
function in `lib.rs`, and DECISIONS §19 has each ISA state the property itself. The
`aarch64` and `sv39` records break the same function in two other ways; the three
patches are not copies.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
index 98df8b66..d8fab919 100644
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -335,7 +335,7 @@ pub enum Half {
 ///
 /// Generic over the format so the split lands at the right bit for the running architecture.
 pub fn is_user_page_va<F: PageFormat>(va: u64) -> bool {
-    F::in_half(Half::Low, va) && va.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE)
+    F::in_half(Half::High, va) && va.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE)
 }
 
